<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naresuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1555">1555</a>–<a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Naresuan the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naresuan was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> from 1590 to 1605. Raised as a hostage at the Burmese court after Ayutthaya fell in 1569, he renounced allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> in 1584, defeated a series of Burmese invasions, and in 1593 won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nong-sarai">Battle of Nong Sarai</a>. He went on to campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and Burma, and is revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> as the restorer of the kingdom's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naresuan.jpg" alt="Naresuan" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mixvasuvadh at Thai Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KingNU.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Prince Naret was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> in 1555–56, the son of King Mahathammarachathirat of Phitsanulok and his queen consort Wisutkasat. His mother was a daughter of Maha Chakkraphat and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriyothai">Suriyothai</a>. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a> took Phitsanulok and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> during the Burmese–Siamese War, he required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammarachathirat">Maha Thammarachathirat</a> to send his son to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Bago</a> as a royal hostage.</p><p>After six years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> about 1570, having followed Burmese military training alongside the sons of princes and nobles. That year his father appointed him Phra Maha Uparaja, or viceroy, at the age of 15, and sent him to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> under the name Naresuan. In 1580 he led 3,000 men against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> force of 5,000 that had set out to raid cities near Ayutthaya, and destroyed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> died in 1581, Naresuan was called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Bago</a> in 1584 to help his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-bayin">Nanda Bayin</a> against a rebellion. According to Prince Damrong, he learned at the border that Burmese forces meant to attack him, and he poured water from a gold goblet to declare that Siam had severed friendship with Hongsawadi. Nanda Bayin's armies later failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, including in a five-month siege in 1586. Naresuan was crowned King of Ayutthaya as Sanphet II in July 1590, after the death of his father.</p><p>His best-known fight came in January 1593 at Nong Sarai, where the Burmese crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-swa">Mingyi Swa</a> was killed. Thai tradition tells of a formal elephant duel in which Naresuan cut down Mingyi Swa with his ngao, or glaive. Burmese chronicles do not mention a duel, and the historian Terwiel concluded that no formal duel probably took place and that Swa was mortally wounded by a gun fired by Naresuan or a man with him. Naresuan afterwards captured Dawei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tanintharyi</a>, took Longvek in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, and became overlord of Lan Na.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1605 Naresuan raised an army of 200,000 men against a threat from the King of Ava. He reached the Fang District of Chiang Mai Province, fell ill, and died three days later on 25 April 1605, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekathotsarot">Ekathotsarot</a> succeeded him. Soon after his death Lan Na and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> broke free of Siamese suzerainty.</p><p>His standing as a national hero was largely cemented in later centuries, especially after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767. Prince Damrong Rajanubhab championed it. Royal Thai Armed Forces Day, 18 January, commemorates his victory in 1593, and Naresuan University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naresuan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Naresuan</b> renounced allegiance in 1584 and defeated successive Burmese invasions; at the Battle of Nong Sarai in 1593 he killed the Burmese crown prince Mingyi Swa, according to the chronicles in single combat on elephant-back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In theory he commanded all manpower and so owned all land; since <b>Naresuan</b> every phrai was bound to him directly.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>After Bayinnaung's death in 1581 the empire fell apart; <b>Naresuan</b> freed Ayutthaya in 1584, and Nanda Bayin's wars exhausted the realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chan Royal Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Wiang Haeng, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.83,100.26&amp;year=1587" aria-label="Chan Royal Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Naresuan"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5835 6079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5835 6079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.83,100.26&amp;year=1587">What was here in 1587? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
